    <meta name="dc.description" content="Environmental pollution from heavy metals, organic contaminants, emerging micro-pollutants, and gaseous species poses serious threats to ecosystems and human health, driving the development of efficient remediation strategies. Adsorption remains one of the most promising approaches due to its operational simplicity, cost-effectiveness, and potential for regeneration. Surface modification of adsorbents has emerged as a key strategy to overcome limitations of traditional adsorbent materials by enhancing surface chemistry, porosity, active functionality, and target-specific interactions. In this review, we first provide an overview of major environmental contaminants and the imperative for advanced remediation techniques, followed by a detailed discussion of surface modification methods, including chemical, physical, biological/green, and nanotechnology-enabled techniques that tailor adsorbent properties for enhanced performance. We then elucidate fundamental mechanisms that govern enhanced adsorption&#8212;electrostatic interactions, ion exchange, surface complexation and &#960;-&#960; interactions&#8212;connecting material design with adsorption outcomes. Recent advances in engineered adsorbent platforms are surveyed, with focus on graphene-based materials, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), bio-based adsorbents, and magnetic composites, highlighting their synthesis, functionalization, and pollutant-specific applications. Practical applications in heavy metal removal, organic pollutant sequestration (including dyes, pesticides, pharmaceuticals, and other emerging contaminants), and gas-phase adsorption (CO&#8322; and volatile organic compounds) are critically assessed. Major challenges such as scalability, cost, stability, regeneration, selectivity, and environmental impact of modified adsorbents are examined, and future directions are proposed, including the integration of artificial intelligence and machine learning for adsorbent optimization and a shift toward sustainable, eco-friendly material design. This review synthesizes key trends and identifies avenues for future research aimed at advancing surface-modified adsorbents for comprehensive environmental remediation. Surface modification enables high-performance, pollutant-specific adsorbents for environmental remediation. Engineered interfacial mechanisms drive enhanced adsorption efficiency. Graphene derivatives, MOFs, bio-based materials, MXenes, and magnetic hybrids exhibit superior capacity and regeneration stability. Surface-engineered adsorbents effectively remove heavy metals, organic pollutants, emerging contaminants, and gaseous species. AI-assisted design and sustainable surface engineering define future directions for scalable, real-world remediation."/>
